## 1. Git workflow (mandatory, no exceptions)

### GitHub — `gregoryfiel/gestao_lancamento_de_chamados`
(Documentation, this rule, coordination docs.)

- Base branch: `main` (only long-lived branch). Always cut new branches from an
  up-to-date `main` (`git pull --ff-only` before branching). When earlier
  `v1.x.y` branches have not yet merged, stack on top of the latest unmerged
  one with `git reset --hard origin/vX.Y.Z` so context is preserved.
- Branch naming: `v1.x.y`
  - Increment `x` for feature, improvement or new logic. Reset `y` to `0`.
  - Increment `y` for fix, hotfix or bug.
- Commits: Conventional Commits in English with a short scope. Allowed types:
  `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `perf`, `build`, `ci`.
- PR target: `main`. Open via `gh pr create` whenever the CLI is installed and
  authenticated. Otherwise push the branch and surface the compare URL.
- Never use destructive git commands (force push, hard reset against shared
  history, branch deletion on remote) without explicit user request.

### Azure DevOps — `data-platform-bees-consumer-mkt-strategy-insights`
(BFF + active SPA at `apps/access-requests-portal/`.)

- Default = create a new branch `feat/access-requests-portal-<topic>` cut from
  `master` (`git fetch origin && git checkout master && git pull --ff-only`).
- Exception: if the user explicitly asks to keep working on an existing feature
  branch (e.g. to add a follow-up commit before review), stay on that branch.
- Conventional Commits in English. PR target: `master`.
- Standing automation policy: the agent MAY commit and push to the working
  feature branch without asking, but **MUST NOT** create or update Pull
  Requests automatically.
- Do not modify shared infrastructure files (`app.yaml`, `requirements.txt`,
  `.gitignore`, `workspace_auth.py`, `ticket_models.py`) unless the task
  explicitly requires it; CRLF/LF noise must not appear in commits.

### Branch-only coordination docs (`docs/coordination/`, GitHub only)

- Per-release Markdown pair: `vX.Y.Z-frontend-sync.md` and
  `vX.Y.Z-backend-sync.md` (plus templates). Used so frontend and backend
  workstreams understand each other on the **same** `v1.x.y` branch.
- **Never merge** `docs/coordination/**` into `main`. Before merging a PR to
  `main`, remove that directory from the diff (e.g.
  `git rm -r docs/coordination`) or exclude it from the squash/cherry-pick.
- Canonical policy: [docs/coordination/README.md](docs/coordination/README.md).

### Deliverable format for every PR (copy-paste ready)

Always return to the user the following four blocks, in this order:

1. PR URL — created via `gh pr create` when possible (GitHub) or the Azure
   DevOps PR creation URL (ADO). When PR creation is out of policy (ADO
   automation rule), provide the compare/branch URL the user can open.
2. Title (English): `vX.Y.Z — <conventional commit subject>` for GitHub, or
   `feat(access-requests-portal): <subject>` for ADO.
3. Body (English) with these sections:
   - `## Summary` — what changed and why.
   - `## Changes` — bullet list of concrete edits and new files.
   - `## Validation` — commands run (lint, build, manual checks) and outcomes.
   - `## Out of scope` — explicit non-goals to prevent review drift.
4. Approval comment in pt-BR, short and direct, ready to paste in the PR.

